      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© The Author 2016. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved. For Permissions, please email: journals.permissions@oup.com.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "Background",
            "content": "Lifestyle factors may be associated with chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy (CIPN). We examined associations between body mass index (BMI) and lifestyle factors with CIPN in the Pathways Study, a prospective cohort of women with invasive breast cancer."
          },
          {
            "Label": "Methods",
            "content": "Analyses included 1237 women who received taxane treatment and provided data on neurotoxicity symptoms. Baseline interviews assessed BMI (normal: <25\u2009kg/m(2); overweight: 25-29.9\u2009kg/m(2); obese: ≥30\u2009kg/m(2)), moderate-to-vigorous physical activity (MVPA) (low: <2.5; medium: 2.5-5; high: >5\u2009hours/week) and fruit/vegetable intake (low: <35 servings/week; high: ≥35 servings/week). Baseline and six-month interviews assessed antioxidant supplement use (nonuser, discontinued, continued user, initiator). CIPN was assessed at baseline, six months, and 24 months using the Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy-Taxane Neurotoxicity (FACT-NTX); a 10% decrease was considered clinically meaningful."
          },
          {
            "Label": "Results",
            "content": "At baseline, 65.6% of patients in the sample were overweight or obese, 29.9% had low MVPA, 57.5% had low fruit/vegetable intake, and 9.5% reported antioxidant supplement use during treatment. In multivariable analyses, increased CIPN was more likely to occur in overweight (odds ratio [OR] = 2.37, 95% confidence interval [CI] = 1.19 to 4.88) and obese patients (OR\u2009=\u20093.21, 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.52 to 7.02) compared with normal weight patients at 24 months and less likely to occur in patients with high MVPA compared with those with low MVPA at six (OR\u2009=\u20090.56, 95% CI\u2009=\u20090.34 to 0.94) and 24 months (OR\u2009=\u20090.43, 95% CI\u2009=\u20090.21 to 0.87). Compared with nonusers, patients who initiated antioxidant use during treatment were more likely to report increased CIPN at six months (OR\u2009=\u20093.81, 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.82 to 8.04)."
          },
          {
            "Label": "Conclusions",
            "content": "Obesity and low MVPA were associated with CIPN in breast cancer patients who received taxane treatment."
          }
        ]
      },
